Chapter 17 — Part 1
Curtis found a stack of takeaway napkins in the kitchen drawer. He folded two of them into simple cranes—quick, precise creases—then wrote on the back of a third.
Thanks for the couch. Looking at the basement listing today. Didn’t want to wake you. – Curtis
He set the note and the cranes on the coffee table, shouldered the duffel, and stepped into the hall. The door clicked shut behind him. A faint telekinetic nudge turned the lock from the outside. The deadbolt slid home with a soft thunk. He headed down the stairs and into the pre-dawn street.
Hours later Hina’s alarm pulled her out of restless sleep. She padded into the living room in an oversized shirt and stopped. The couch was empty. Two paper cranes sat beside a note. Yui emerged from her room a minute later, hair messy, and read over Hina’s shoulder.
“He left.” Yui’s voice was still thick with sleep. “And locked the door behind him.”
Hina picked up one of the cranes, cheeks warm even without him in the room. “He looked at the listing.” She traced the fold with her thumb. “And he didn’t want to wake us.”
They stood there a moment longer, the apartment quieter than it had been at midnight, the low echo of Free Love still sitting under their skin while the morning light finally reached the coffee table.
Hina sat on the arm of the couch with the paper crane still in her hands. Morning light cut across the empty cushions. Yui dropped into the opposite chair, scrolling the listing Curtis had been sent, one knee drawn up.
“He really locked it from outside,” Yui said. “Telekinesis or just really quiet hands?”
“Does it matter?” Hina turned the crane over. The note was short. Practical. Exactly like him. “He’s looking at the basement. That means he might actually stay close.”
Yui’s mouth curved. The warmth from last night hadn’t fully left either of them. “You hoping he takes it?”
“Yeah.” Hina didn’t bother lying. Her cheeks stayed pink. “He was on our couch. Bandaged. And he still got up at whatever hour and left without waking us. I keep thinking about—” She stopped, shook her head. “Never mind.”
“About climbing him like a tree. I know.” Yui laughed under her breath. “Same. He’s dense as hell about it, though. Didn’t even blink when we both told him to stay.”
Hina set the crane down carefully. “If he moves in downstairs, we’re going to see him all the time.”
“Terrible,” Yui said, grinning. “Absolutely terrible for productivity.”
They sat with the quiet a while longer, the note between them, both of them already checking the time and wondering whether the basement suite would still be open by the time Curtis finished whatever he was doing before class.
Curtis was three blocks from the station when his phone buzzed. The screen was cracked from the USJ, spiderwebbing across Momo’s name. He answered anyway.
“Y’ello?”
“Beardy. It’s Yaoyorozu.” Her voice was clear, composed. “Aizawa sent a message through the class group. We have the day off. Yesterday’s incident requires facility repairs and faculty review. No classes.”
Curtis slowed on the sidewalk. “Neat. But how’d you get my number?”
A short pause. “Iida compiled an emergency contact list after he became representative. You were added when you transferred. I hope that’s acceptable.”
“It’s fine.” He glanced at the cracked screen. “Thanks for the heads-up. I’ll stay out of trouble.”
“Please do.” Her tone softened a fraction. “And… we’re glad you’re all right. The class has been asking.”
“Tell them I’m vertical. See you tomorrow.”
“Tomorrow, then.”
The call ended. Curtis pocketed the phone and kept walking, the morning still early, the basement listing open on his screen, and a full day suddenly free in front of him.
