The chat kept moving. Momo locked her phone, expression perfectly neutral, and pretended she had not just committed light registry fraud before breakfast.
The club was dark when Curtis pushed the side door open. Morning cleaning lights only. Janice and Tatsuya were at the bar going over the night’s receipts. Both looked up at the sound of the door.
Janice’s expression softened the second she saw him vertical. “You look like hell. But you’re walking.”
Tatsuya set the tablet down. “Kid. We got the texts. Didn’t expect you to actually show up.”
Curtis stopped at the end of the bar, duffel still on his shoulder. “Wanted to check in. And ask for a shift. Got the day off from school. Not much else to do.”
Janice shook her head before he finished. “No.”
“I can handle a short—”
“No,” she repeated, firmer. “You were on a stretcher yesterday. You’re not working my floor today.” She nodded toward the door. “Go enjoy Japan. Eat something that isn’t café ramen. Sleep in a real bed if you find one. Come back tomorrow if you’re still standing.”
Tatsuya nodded. “She’s right. We’ll keep your weekend slot. Just don’t make us watch another pixelated news photo.”
Curtis’s mouth twitched. Almost a smile. “Alright. I’ll stay out of trouble.”
“Liar,” Janice said, but she was smiling. “Get out of here, Beardy.”
He bowed once, short, and stepped back into the morning street. The door swung shut behind him. The day was suddenly open, and the low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while he decided what to do with it.
Curtis slowed outside a corner café with wide windows and too many plants. Something sweet drifted through the open door—berries, sugar, warm pastry. He stepped inside on instinct.
The display case held rows of perfect slices. A handwritten card sat in front of the brightest one.
“Strawberry cheesecake?”
He made a face. Cheese on a cake. The idea sat wrong. Back home, cheese belonged on bread or in a skillet, not under fruit and glaze. It looked like an abomination dressed up for city people.
He ordered it anyway.
The server set the slice down with a tiny fork and a smile. Curtis stared at it for a second, then cut a corner and tried it. Sweet. Creamy. The strawberry cut through the richness. Not what he expected. Not bad.
He took another bite, slower. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the fancy café hummed around him and he worked through the whole slice like it might explain itself if he finished it.
The waitress returned with a tall glass and set it beside the empty plate. “Sweet tea. On the house. You looked like you needed it.”
Curtis blinked. Condensation ran down the side. He lifted it, took a sip, and his eyes lit—both of them, red and blue bright under the café lights.
Sweet. Cold. Real sugar, not the rare hard candy or wild honey he’d stretched for weeks in the mountains. Back in the forest, sugar had been an occasion. Something you found, rationed, remembered. It hadn’t occurred to him, until the glass was in his hand, that he could just… have it. Whenever he wanted. Walk into a place, ask, pay, and there it was.
He took another long drink. The ice clicked. A small, genuine smile pulled at his mouth.
“Thanks,” he said, accent low. “It’s really good.”
The waitress smiled and left him to it. Curtis sat with the glass between his bandaged hand and the other, working through the sweet tea like it was the first easy thing the day had offered, while the city moved past the window and the low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet.
Mina was cutting through the side street with a shopping bag when she glanced through the café window and stopped.
Curtis sat at the small table by the glass, compression shirt under the open jacket, bandaged hand loose around a tall glass of sweet tea. He was smiling—gentle, unguarded, the kind that reached both eyes. He took another slow bite of what was left of the cheesecake, then a sip, and the heterochromia caught the light with something open and almost child-like. No tension in his shoulders. No flat practical mask. Just a guy enjoying sugar like it was new.
Mina’s usual grin softened. She watched for a second longer, pink cheeks warm, then pushed the door open and slipped inside.
“Didn’t know you could look that happy over cake,” she said, sliding into the seat across from him without asking. “Save me a bite or I’m telling the whole class.”
Curtis looked up, the soft expression still there. “Mina. Hey. It’s strawberry. Weird, but good.”
She stole a forkful anyway, eyes bright. “You’re ridiculous. Also, day off means you’re coming to the arcade with us later. No arguments.”
He huffed a quiet laugh and nudged the plate toward her. The child-like shine didn’t fade while she talked, and the low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet between them.
Curtis nudged the plate a little closer, still smiling that soft, open smile. “So did you know they put cheese on a cake? Sounds gross. But this is delicious.”
Mina laughed, bright and easy, and stole another bite. “It’s not regular cheese, you mountain cryptid. It’s cream cheese. Sweetened, mixed with eggs and sugar, baked so it sets. Then they throw fruit on it so it doesn’t feel like you’re eating a brick of dairy.” She grinned. “Welcome to cheesecake. City people have been doing this for ages.”
Curtis took another slow forkful, considering. “Cream cheese. Huh. Still weird. Still good.”
“Stick with me and you’ll try worse,” Mina said, already waving the waitress over for a second fork. “Now finish that tea. We’re meeting the others in an hour and you’re not allowed to look this peaceful without backup.”
He huffed a quiet laugh and kept eating, the child-like shine still in his eyes while Mina kept talking and the café hummed around them.
Mina didn’t give him time to finish the last bite. She hooked her arm through his good one and tugged him up from the table. “Come on. You’re officially kidnapped. Day off means you’re not allowed to wander alone looking like a kicked puppy who discovered sugar.”
Curtis let himself be pulled, duffel over his shoulder, the soft smile still lingering. “Wasn’t wandering. Was eating.”
“Same thing.”
They hit the sidewalk and made it two blocks before the rest of the group appeared around the corner—Uraraka, Jiro, Hagakure’s floating gloves, and Kaminari trailing with a drink. Mina lit up and swung their joined arms like a prize.
“Look who I found!”
Uraraka’s cheeks went pink on sight. “Curtis! You’re out—are you supposed to be out?”
Jiro’s earjacks twitched, a small smirk forming. “Cafe hostage situation. Should’ve guessed.”
Kaminari grinned. “Dude, you look way better than the stretcher photo. Mina, you’re sharing, right?”
Curtis lifted his free hand in a short wave, expression easy. “Hey. Mina decided I needed supervision.”
“Correct,” Mina said, already steering the whole group toward the arcade down the street. “Now move. We’ve got a day off and a cryptid to corrupt. Let’s go.”
They fell in around him, voices overlapping, Uraraka drifting a little closer on his other side while the city opened up in front of them.
Jiro fell in on Curtis’s other side as they walked. Her earjacks twitched toward the bandaged hand Mina wasn’t holding.
“Hey. What happened to your hand? You didn’t have that yesterday.”
Curtis glanced at the wrap, then shrugged. “Oh. I got stabbed last night. After I got out of the hospital.”
The group stuttered.
Uraraka’s eyes went wide. “Stabbed?!”
Kaminari nearly dropped his drink. “Dude, what?”
Mina stopped dead, arm still linked with his. “You were out for six hours and already got stabbed? What the hell, Curtis?”
“Two guys were bothering Hina. One came back with a knife.” He flexed the fingers once. The bandage held. “Caught it. They’re gone. It’s fine.”
Jiro stared at him. “You caught a knife. With your hand. After fighting a Nomu.”
“Wasn’t the pointy end I was worried about.” His tone stayed even. “Hina’s okay. That’s the part that mattered.”
Uraraka’s cheeks were pink again, but her voice came out small. “You really just… walked it off?”
“Pretty much.” Curtis started walking again. Mina muttered something about cryptid immune systems and tightened her grip on his arm. The rest of the group followed, still processing, while the arcade signs got closer and the low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet.
Kaminari snorted as they started moving again. “Okay, yeah. Caught a knife, fought a Nomu, drinks his own blood probably. You’re actually a cryptid. Officially.”
Mina grinned. “Mountain cryptid confirmed. We should get him a plaque.”
Uraraka laughed, soft. “Does that mean we have to leave out offerings now?”
Jiro didn’t join the jokes right away. She reached over, careful, and gently took Curtis’s bandaged hand in both of hers. Her thumbs rested light on either side of the wrap, earjacks still. She turned it just enough to check the edges of the bandage, then looked up at him.
“Next time just dodge,” she said, quieter. “Cryptid or not.”
Curtis blinked, surprised by the contact, but didn’t pull away. “I’ll keep it in mind.”
She held on for another second, then let go, a faint flush on her cheeks. Mina caught it and smirked but didn’t call it out. The group kept walking toward the arcade, the joke still bouncing between them, while Jiro stayed a half-step closer and the low, steady pull in Curtis’s chest remained as quiet as ever.
The arcade lights hit them as soon as they pushed through the doors—neon, noise, the smell of fried snacks and carpet cleaner. Mina still had Curtis’s arm hooked like a prize. Jiro walked on his other side, close enough that her shoulder brushed his every few steps. Uraraka drifted just ahead, glancing back with that soft pink still on her cheeks. Kaminari and Hagakure’s gloves led the way toward the rhythm games.
“First round’s on me if you can actually play,” Mina announced. “Cryptid privileges don’t cover being bad at DDR.”
Curtis looked at the machines, expression easy. “Never tried. Mountains didn’t have these.”
“Tragic,” Jiro said, mouth twitching. “We’ll fix it.”
Kaminari slapped a machine. “Loser buys the next round of drinks. Uraraka, you’re with me. Mina and Curtis versus me and Jiro. Hagakure’s judging because she cheats.”
“I do not!” the gloves protested.
Uraraka laughed and stepped up to her pad. Mina finally released Curtis’s arm so he could take the one beside her. He rolled his shoulders, bandaged hand loose, and watched the arrows scroll for a second like he was reading trail markers.
“Alright,” he said. “Show me.”
The music kicked in. The group crowded around the machines, voices overlapping, the day-off noise swallowing the last of the morning quiet while Curtis stepped onto the pad and the low, steady pull in his chest stayed exactly where it always was.
Mina had queued the song on easy. The arrows crawled. Curtis missed the first three on purpose, then the fourth by accident, boots barely shifting.
“It’s too slow.”
He reached over and cranked the difficulty before anyone could stop him. Expert+. Both pads lit up. The machine beeped in protest. He scrolled, found the DDR remix of Butterfly by Smile.dk, and locked it in.
The arrows exploded across both screens.
Curtis set his feet—one on each pad—and moved. No wasted motion. Heel, toe, spin, recover. Every note landed perfect. The combo counter climbed so fast the machine stuttered. “PERFECT” stacked until the screen flashed gold. Halfway through, a bonus prompt popped in the corner: MARRY ME! He hit the corresponding arrows without breaking rhythm and the message exploded across the display in pink hearts.
The high-score board rewrote itself in real time. First place. Then it kept climbing.
Mina’s mouth hung open. Jiro’s earjacks had gone rigid. Kaminari was yelling. Uraraka had both hands on her cheeks. Hagakure’s gloves were flailing.
Curtis finished the last run clean, chest barely rising, and stepped off the pads as the machine screamed new records. He looked at the group, expression easy.
“Mountains didn’t have these. But the timing’s the same as not getting hit.”
Mina finally found her voice. “You’re banned from easy mode forever. Also, I’m marrying the machine if it asks me next.”
The arcade noise swallowed her laugh while the screen behind Curtis kept flashing his name at the top of the list.
The machine was still screaming new records when the group found their voices.
Mina grabbed Curtis’s arm again, shaking it. “You— you just— Expert Plus! Both pads! I put it on easy so you wouldn’t embarrass yourself and you speedran the entire song! I’m telling everyone. Forever.”
Jiro’s earjacks were still stiff. She stared at the screen, then at him. “The timing on the spins shouldn’t even be possible. You didn’t miss a single off-beat.” A flush crept up her neck. “That’s disgusting. Do it again.”
Uraraka had floated an inch off the floor without noticing, hands glued to her cheeks. “You got the marry-me prompt! Those are so hard—I’ve never even seen one in person! Curtis, that was amazing!”
Kaminari was doubled over laughing. “Bro just proposed to the machine and it said yes! High score’s ruined for the rest of us. I’m never playing against you.”
Hagakure’s gloves waved wildly. “Did you see the combo counter?! It broke! It actually broke! Cryptid rhythm god confirmed!”
Even a couple of random arcade kids had stopped to watch the board rewrite itself. Curtis stood there, bandaged hand loose at his side, expression easy while the group crowded closer and the machine behind him kept flashing his name in first place like it wasn’t sure how he’d done it either.
Curtis’s phone buzzed in his pocket. He stepped off the pad and answered, cracked screen lighting with Hina’s name.
“Hey, Beardy-kun? It’s Hina. The landlord’s on the property and would like to meet you. About the basement suite.”
He glanced at the group still crowded around the machine. “Okay. I’ll head back over.”
“We’ll wait by the entrance. Don’t rush if you’re busy.”
“I’m not. See you soon.”
He hung up. Mina was already grinning. “Landlord? You’re actually getting a place?”
“Maybe.” Curtis shouldered the duffel. “Thanks for the kidnapping. I’ll catch the next round.”
Uraraka waved, still pink. “Text us if you get it!”
Jiro nodded. “And don’t get stabbed on the way.”
He huffed a short laugh, gave them a small wave, and headed for the doors. The arcade noise faded behind him as he stepped back into the afternoon street, already turning toward the station that would take him to Hina’s building.
The arcade doors swung shut behind him. The group stayed by the machine for a second, still riding the high of the high score.
Mina’s grin faded first. “Wait.”
Jiro’s earjacks twitched. “Where has he been sleeping since he got to Japan?”
Uraraka’s eyes widened. “He never had a place. He—he’s been at school, then the hospital, then… where did he go at night?”
Kaminari blinked. “The net café? He mentioned one once. Those booths aren’t—”
“This whole time?” Hagakure’s gloves had gone still. “No apartment? No dorm? Just… wherever?”
Mina’s mouth tightened. She looked toward the doors he’d just walked through. “And he was smiling over cheesecake like it was the best part of his week. We didn’t even ask.”
The machine behind them kept flashing his name in first place. None of them were looking at it anymore. Uraraka’s hands had curled against her chest, the soft pink on her cheeks replaced by something quieter and worried, while the rest of the group stood in the neon noise realizing how long he’d been without a real bed.
